  "summary": "Knuckles' Chaotix is Sega's platform game, first released for 32X in Japan on 21 April 1995.",
  "body": [
    "Knuckles' Chaotix is Sega's platform game, first released for 32X in Japan on 21 April 1995. Knuckles and a group of allies try to stop Doctor Robotnik and Metal Sonic from exploiting the power of mysterious rings. Its stages retain the series' rings, enemies and bright mechanical landscapes, but movement revolves around two characters connected by an elastic tether. A partner can be controlled by the computer or another player. Their relationship becomes the main tool for crossing the stage, replacing a solitary run with movement that must account for another body.",
    "The connection behaves like a rubber band, allowing one character's position and momentum to affect the other. Handling it successfully requires judging both figures rather than only the one directly controlled. Their individual abilities add further possibilities: Knuckles glides and climbs, Mighty wall-jumps, Espio moves along walls and ceilings, Vector boosts through the air and Charmy flies. A useful pairing therefore changes how the tether can be employed around the landscape. The stage becomes a space for arranging the two characters before releasing the movement built between them.",
    "The tether idea began in the Mega Drive prototype Sonic Crackers before the project moved to 32X and adopted its final cast. Junko Shiratsu and Mariko Nanba composed the score, accompanying one of the add-on's most distinctive original releases. The unusual physics divided players, while several of its characters later returned elsewhere in the series. Its identity remains tied to that experiment in partnership: familiar platform elements are still present, but even an ordinary jump or burst of speed depends on understanding where the companion is and how the connection will respond."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "Two characters joined by a ring",
    "Movement through a partner",
    "A different branch of Sonic"
  ],
